WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.300
Hospitals bill $4,000 for an MRI who's installed scanner cost $2 million. This is First Principles

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:13.680
Daily, Episode 170. A typical three Tesla magnetic resonance imaging scanner, an MRI, reaches

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.960
a hospital with a turnkey price between $2.8 and $4.5 million, once shielding, cooling,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.160
and integration are added. Scans from that machine show up on patient

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:32.100
bills at $2,500 to $4,500 each. The hospital's own internal cost per scan often sits between

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:39.720
$420 and $750. The gap between those figures is not the weight of niobium and it is not the price

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:45.760
of helium. It is how the machine is bought, housed, scheduled and marked up. Financing,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.220
facility overhead and charge master pricing sit on top of the scanner itself.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:55.240
health. Most facilities never reach the volume that would thin that stack because of how

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:00.860
rooms are booked and how referrals arrive. Hospitals take scanners on capital leases or

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:06.600
operating leases that spread payments over 7 to 10 years and add interest of $200,000

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.940
to $500,000 per machine. Few institutions pay cash for a multi-million-dollar asset whose

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:17.880
useful life is measured in a decade or more, so the lease is the path that gets the magnet

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:18.500
it in the door.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.200
Site work lands before the first patient.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.760
A ferridae cage, dedicated electrical service, and chilled water cooling together add hundreds

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:27.880
of thousands of dollars.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:33.340
Each piece exists because the superconducting magnet must be isolated from radio frequency

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.160
interference and kept at cryogenic temperatures.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:43.240
Once the room is running, an annual service contract of $100,000 to $250,000 covers parts,

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:48.400
labor and guaranteed uptime, and it locks the hospital into the manufacturer's

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.960
ecosystem for the life of the lease. Electricity is separate. Occasional

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.980
helium refills and older systems can reach $80,000. Throughput decides how

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:02.420
thinly those fixed costs spread. A center running 12,000 scans a year

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:09.080
amortizes the capital at roughly $25 per scan. A room limited to 3,500 scans

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:15.780
carries $86 per scan in capital alone. Most US hospitals do not operate at the higher volume.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:21.120
Scheduling, staffing, and referral patterns are built around single-machine rooms rather than

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:27.460
high-throughput imaging centers. Facility fees, radiologist interpretation, and administrative overhead

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:33.080
are layered on after that, and the charge master list price is set far above any of those internal

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:39.000
costs. The bill feels routine inside the industry because every participant has adapted to the same

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.880
financing, regulatory, and scheduling constraints rather than questioning them.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.920
One objection is that the magnet itself must be the expensive part.

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:53.580
The sources show the opposite about what keeps costing money after purchase, service contracts,

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.380
helium logistics, and underused rooms, not the raw cost of the superconducting wire.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.780
This is where the show usually builds a magic wand number.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.280
Wave a wand, arrange the raw atoms into the finished scanner for free, and the only bill

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.220
left is the materials. That floor is the denominator of the first principles ratio.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:19.080
Finish price divided by raw material cost. A ratio near one means the thing is already

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:24.937
close to its physical limit. A large ratio means most of the price is design, process,

00:03:25.257 --> 00:03:29.957
organization, or rules. And that is where a from scratch rethink has room to work.

00:03:30.577 --> 00:03:35.277
The ratio measures where the price sits. It is not a verdict on the people who built the machine.

00:03:35.277 --> 00:03:41.277
The research sources for this episode list purchase prices, installation costs, and per

00:03:41.277 --> 00:03:42.697
scan internal expenses.

00:03:43.137 --> 00:03:47.617
They do not break out commodity prices or quantities for the raw materials inside the

00:03:47.617 --> 00:03:50.717
magnet, the gradient coils, or the electronics.

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:57.237
No massive niobium, no helium at a stated commodity price, no quantities for the rest of the

00:03:57.237 --> 00:03:58.837
wire, the steel, or the boards.

00:03:59.497 --> 00:04:03.717
Without those inputs, there is nothing to add up in words and nothing to divide a finished

00:04:03.717 --> 00:04:09.277
price by. A magic wand floor cannot be built from the given data, and neither can a first

00:04:09.277 --> 00:04:14.437
principles ratio. What the sources do show is where the money goes after the scanner itself.

00:04:15.077 --> 00:04:20.557
Site work, financing, and low utilization dominate. Not an irreducible physical input.

00:04:21.097 --> 00:04:25.537
The remaining gap up to the build price sits in scheduling rules, facility overhead,

00:04:26.037 --> 00:04:32.037
and pricing opacity, not in the atoms of the machine. Could helium or niobium still take over the

00:04:32.037 --> 00:04:33.617
bill if every room ran full.

00:04:34.257 --> 00:04:39.077
The sources indicate that newer sealed systems have already reduced helium consumption dramatically,

00:04:39.617 --> 00:04:42.817
which shifts the burden further toward those non-material layers.

00:04:43.477 --> 00:04:48.657
Another objection is that regulatory requirements for shielded rooms are fixed, so the building

00:04:48.657 --> 00:04:49.597
work cannot move.

00:04:50.177 --> 00:04:54.277
The portable systems that follow show those requirements can be bypassed when field

00:04:54.277 --> 00:04:55.177
strength is lowered.

00:04:55.797 --> 00:05:01.730
The present cost structure is not dictated solely by physics, hyperfine, founded by Jonathan

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:07.890
in Rothburg has shipped more than 100 swoop portable scanners at about $250,000 each,

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:15.870
20 times less than a conventional 1.5 Tesla system. The 0.064 Tesla device needs no cryogens,

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:22.070
no dedicated shielded room, and only standard wall power. That removes the largest installation

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:27.370
and operating expenses a conventional magnet carries. In a remote Canadian hospital,

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:36.010
the portable unit cut per patient imaging costs from $26,105 to $9,000-$8,000, largely by eliminating

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:44.710
air transport. The one-year saving for 50 patients reached $854,841. Those savings come because

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:49.690
the scanner travels to the patient instead of requiring the patient to travel to a fixed installation.

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:53.930
Transport and lodging, the lines that dominate remote care budgets,

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:55.510
collapse with that reversal.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:06:01.210
A circular procurement model studied by Vanderbilt and Phillips keeps 92% of the materials

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:06.690
in an existing three-testless scanner when it is upgraded on-site, trimming total ownership

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:12.530
costs by up to 21% or $420,000 to $520,000 per machine.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:17.810
The upgrade reuses the magnet and the gradient hardware while refreshing electronics and

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:22.370
software, avoiding the full cost of a new shielded room and a new installation.

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:29.150
These moves attack the same cost layers, site infrastructure, transport, and one-time replacement cycles.

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:34.810
Further progress would require regulatory acceptance of lower field images for more indications,

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:42.530
larger production volumes of the portable systems, and payment models that reward throughput instead of per-scan facility fees.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:50.510
The hard constraints remain image quality for certain diagnoses, and the capital still required to manufacture even the simpler magnet.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:56.110
Lowering field strength trades some resolution for dramatically lower infrastructure, so the

00:06:56.110 --> 00:07:00.370
portable route expands access rather than displacing every high field scanner.

00:07:01.396 --> 00:07:05.976
One concrete change in field strength and cryogen requirement can eliminate an entire

00:07:05.976 --> 00:07:09.196
category of building work that had been treated as fixed.

00:07:09.616 --> 00:07:14.796
The shielded room, the chilled water cooling, the dedicated power, the helium logistics,

00:07:15.316 --> 00:07:18.576
that package came to look like the price of having a magnet at all.

00:07:18.576 --> 00:07:24.136
It is the price of a high-field superconducting magnet that must be isolated from radio-frequency

00:07:24.136 --> 00:07:26.956
interference and held at cryogenic temperatures.

00:07:27.536 --> 00:07:32.716
Jonathan Rothberg's machines take the other premise, lower the field, remove the cryogens,

00:07:33.316 --> 00:07:36.236
and that package is no longer a prerequisite for a scan.

00:07:36.896 --> 00:07:41.656
Image quality still limits which diagnoses can move, so this does not retire the three

00:07:41.656 --> 00:07:42.296
Tesla room.

00:07:42.816 --> 00:07:44.296
It shows the room was a choice.

00:07:44.296 --> 00:07:49.436
A second change in the ownership model can keep most of the original materials in service

00:07:49.436 --> 00:07:51.976
instead of sending them to scrap after a decade.

00:07:52.536 --> 00:07:56.216
The financing already assumes a useful life measured in a decade or more.

00:07:56.776 --> 00:08:00.776
The procurement habit does not have to answer that lifespan with a full replacement.

00:08:01.476 --> 00:08:05.836
Vanderbilt and Phillips studied an on-site upgrade that leaves the heavy hardware working

00:08:05.836 --> 00:08:07.836
and refreshes what actually dates.

00:08:08.476 --> 00:08:11.116
Scrap in that picture is a contract decision.

00:08:11.116 --> 00:08:15.996
The same arithmetic that lowered the cost of portable units can be applied next to the

00:08:15.996 --> 00:08:20.056
scheduling and billing layers that still multiply the price of every scan.

00:08:20.456 --> 00:08:25.496
A scanner can get cheaper to buy, cheaper to house, and cheaper to keep, and still meet

00:08:25.496 --> 00:08:28.456
a list price set far above the hospital's internal cost.

00:08:28.996 --> 00:08:33.776
A facility fee attached to the room, and a calendar that never fills the way a high-volume

00:08:33.776 --> 00:08:35.056
center's calendar fills.

00:08:35.616 --> 00:08:38.236
Those multipliers do not care what the magnet cost.

00:08:38.236 --> 00:08:41.296
They sit on top of whatever scan comes out of it.

00:08:41.616 --> 00:08:45.176
The missing public split is what still blocks a magic wand number.

00:08:45.656 --> 00:08:50.036
Who will publish the first public ledger that separates the scanner's material and manufacturing

00:08:50.036 --> 00:08:53.576
costs from the facility and financing overhead that follows it?

00:08:54.076 --> 00:08:55.916
That's First Principles Daily for Today.

